//! @yah:next("The last mile of W338. A `supply = \"self\"` requirement carries the provider's spec in `Requirement::provides` (R860-T1). On deploy of the requirer, the provider must be stood up first, on the node the locality demands, and gated Ready before the requirer starts (R860-T2's gate is what does the gating). On teardown, cascade along `self` edges and NEVER along `wait` edges — a `wait` provider belongs to whoever declared it.")
//! @yah:verify("cargo test -p yubaba --lib deploy")
//! @yah:gotcha("CONTENTION: the deploy handler lives in oss/yubaba/crates/yubaba/src/lib.rs, which is uncommitted-dirty and adjacent to files @Ashguard:eclipse holds live on R858 (headscale_appliance.rs, appliance_ownership.rs, headscale_state.rs, litestream.rs, leader.rs). Re-check camp.roster and coordinate before claiming. Each member keeps its OWN mesh identity and its own service record — do not collapse the group under one identity, which is W338's load-bearing constraint (§\\\"Each member keeps its own mesh identity\\\").")
//! @arch:see(.yah/docs/working/W338-workload-dependencies-and-appliance-composition.md)
//! @yah:depends_on(R860-T2)
//! @yah:next("INHERITED FROM R860-T4, which deliberately left it rather than reach into a file with three live sessions: node-side drain is still PER-WORKLOAD. `drain_workloads` (oss/yubaba/crates/yubaba/src/lib.rs, the R572-F4 archetype_registry skip around :3117-3128) knows nothing about requirement edges, so a Server bound to an Appliance by a `local` edge would still be drained alone — which defeats the group invariant T4 just established on the placement side. The fix needs group membership plumbed to the node process, which is exactly this ticket's rail. `cloud::config::group_is_drainable(members)` (config.rs:2172) already exists and `yubaba` already depends on `cloud`, so the predicate is directly callable once the plumbing lands — do not reimplement it.")
//! @yah:handoff("BOTH PARTS LANDED, PLUS THE R860-T4 INHERITED DRAIN ITEM. Part 1 (`supply = \\\"self\\\"` provisioning): `deploy_workload_spec` now stands every carried provider up before the R860-B3 gate and before the requirer, in `oss/yubaba/crates/yubaba/src/lib.rs`. Part 2 (teardown cascade): `destroy_workload` cascades along `self` edges only and reports what it took as a new `cascaded` field on the destroy response. Inherited item: `drain_workloads` now consults the placement group, not just the per-workload archetype. New module `oss/yubaba/crates/yubaba/src/deploy/self_supply.rs` holds the pure helpers; new `ServerState.requirement_graph` field is the rail that plumbs group membership to the node process.")
//! @yah:handoff("PROVISIONING IS NODE-SIDE AND RE-ENTERS THE DEPLOY HANDLER — the decision the brief left open. A provider is deployed by calling `deploy_workload_spec` again with its own spec (via `deploy_workload_spec_boxed`, a `Pin<Box<dyn Future>>` erasure that breaks the async-fn self-type cycle; recursion is bounded at one level because `validate::shape` forbids a `provides` spec from itself self-provisioning). That is what satisfies W338's load-bearing constraint rather than merely claiming to: the provider gets its OWN admission check, secret materialization, archetype-registry entry and service record, so it stays independently discoverable and the group is never collapsed under one identity. A helper that shortcut to `rt.deploy_workload` would have collapsed it. Node-side also means every client gets it (CLI, desktop, reconciler), and locality needs no branch — the provider lands on whichever node took the requirer's deploy, which is what `local` demands and `prefer-local` prefers. Teardown mirrors it exactly via `destroy_workload_boxed`.")
//! @yah:gotcha("R860-T4'S HANDOFF SAID \\\"yubaba already depends on cloud\\\" AND IT DOES NOT — `cloud` is a **dev-dependency** of yubaba (oss/yubaba/crates/yubaba/Cargo.toml:152, under `[dev-dependencies]`), so `cloud::config::group_is_drainable` is unreachable from the yubaba lib. `cargo check` proved it: E0433 `unresolved module or unlinked crate cloud`. Adding the real edge would reverse a deliberate decision — cloud's own manifest at Cargo.toml:76-77 records that `local-driver` was moved out of cloud in R374-F3 \\\"so yubaba could own MinIO lifecycle without a reverse yubaba→cloud dep\\\". RESOLUTION, honouring the instruction not to reimplement: the body moved DOWN to `workload_spec::group_is_drainable` (oss/yah-base/crates/workload-spec/src/lib.rs, just above the R860-T1 Requirements section), the crate both sides already depend on. `cloud::config::group_is_drainable` keeps its exact signature and doc and now delegates in one line — every existing caller and T4's tests are untouched. One implementation, no drift, no new dependency edge.")
//! @yah:handoff("DISCOVERED WORK DONE IN THIS PASS: THE B3 GATE WAS BLIND TO `requires`. `await_dependencies` and `compute_dependency_deadline` read `spec.depends_on` directly — exactly what `effective_requirements()`'s own doc forbids (\\\"reading either alone silently drops half the requirements of any spec that uses both\\\") — so a `requires`-declared dependency was gated on nothing and the ticket's premise that B3's gate would block the requirer was false as written. Both now go through a new `mesh_resolve::gated_idents(spec)`. It reads `effective_requirements()` and excludes exactly two classes, each for a stated reason: `Supply::SelfProvision` (already stood up synchronously above, so nothing to wait for — and waiting would DEADLOCK a portless Job provider like W338's `headscale-restore`, since a workload with no mesh ports publishes no service record at all and the requirer would time out against its own sidecar), and `Locality::PreferLocal` (\\\"never blocks placement\\\" is that value's defining property; a node-local registry cannot distinguish \\\"nowhere\\\" from \\\"on another node\\\"). `Local` and `Anywhere` pass through as presence checks. A `depends_on`-only spec is bit-for-bit unaffected — every entry folds in as anywhere+wait. Nothing here forecloses R860-T2's Ready-gating or locality axes.")
//! @yah:handoff("FOUR MORE DECISIONS MADE RATHER THAN ASKED, each documented at its site. (1) ROLLBACK: if the requirer fails at any point AFTER its providers came up — the B3 gate, mesh-env resolution, the backend, or the operator-bridge preauth — `rollback_self_supplied` tears the providers back down in reverse order. A `self` provider exists only to serve its requirer, so a requirer that never started must not leave one running. (2) IDEMPOTENT REDEPLOY: a provider already in `archetype_registry` is skipped, not redeployed — otherwise an Appliance provider's single-instance guard would answer 409 and take the requirer's redeploy down with it. Covered by a test. (3) SAMPLED PRE-MATERIALIZATION: `carried_providers` and `placement_group` are read off the spec beside `authored_archetype`, before secret materialization, because materialization appends bind volumes and `effective_archetype()` infers `Appliance` from a non-empty `volumes` — reading the materialized spec would mark every secret-mounting group non-drainable. That is the R854 trap one layer up. (4) NODE-SIDE GROUP VIEW: `self_supply::local_group_members` rather than `cloud::config::placement_group(ws, &[])`. A node holds no `.yah/infra/workloads/` inventory, so cloud's ident-lookup arm can never fire and its closure degenerates to exactly this list; documented as such, with the same cost cloud documents — a `local` member declared in another file is skipped, and its drain protection stays camp-side.")
