//! @yah:verify("THE THREE BEHAVIOURS THE BRIEF DEMANDED ARE PROVEN END-TO-END THROUGH THE REAL ROUTER, not against the helpers — every one drives `POST /workloads/deploy` / `POST /workloads/{ident}/destroy` and asserts on what the BACKEND was handed, via a `RecordingRuntime` extended to record teardowns as well as deploys. All 12 confirmed passing BY NAME, not just by count. (1) `a_self_supplied_provider_is_deployed_before_its_requirer` — backend deploy order is exactly [provider, requirer]. (2) `a_wait_provider_is_never_deployed_by_its_requirer` — order is [requirer] alone, with the waited-on provider pre-seeded in the service records. (3) `destroy_cascades_into_self_providers_and_leaves_wait_providers_standing` — both halves in one test because the claim is a distinction: teardown order is [requirer, self-provider], `cascaded` reports exactly `[\\\"headscale-replicator\\\"]`, the self provider is fully deregistered, and the `wait` provider is neither torn down nor deregistered. Plus `each_group_member_keeps_its_own_identity_and_service_record` (W338's load-bearing constraint, asserted directly on the two service records), `a_requires_declared_wait_provider_that_never_appears_refuses_the_deploy` (the widened gate actually gates), `a_redeploy_does_not_stand_an_already_live_provider_up_again`, `a_deployed_group_records_the_membership_drain_consults`, and 5 unit tests in self_supply.rs including drainability from BOTH directions (requirer and provider).")
